#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wt — worktree helper

Designed to make it easy to work with multiple Claude Code agents at the same
time.

- Requires no special worktree set up in repo.
- Opens new worktrees in a new terminal tab on macOS — a tab in the current
  cmux workspace (when run inside cmux) or a Ghostty tab — but fails
  gracefully otherwise.
- Finds every worktree git knows about, wherever it lives on disk — including
  ones created by other tools or by hand.
- Works with bare repos (worktrees at git repo root).
- Customizable per-repo for the branch prefix.

Some useful workflows:

- Start a new task in a background tree:  wt co -b <name>
- Foreground a task to current worktree:  wt foreground <worktree name>
- Background a task out of the current worktree:  wt background <new worktree name>
- See all worktrees + their status:  wt list

Subcommands:
  checkout|co   [-b] <branch> [--no-tab|--workspace]
                                       check out a branch in a new worktree (-b creates)
  cob           <branch> [--no-tab|--workspace]
                                       shorthand for `co -b` (create + check out)
  open|o        <name> [--workspace]   open a worktree in a new terminal tab
  background|bg [name] [--no-tab|--workspace]
                                       relocate current branch + changes to a new worktree
  list|l        [--json] [--paths]     show worktrees + change/PR markers
  delete|d      <name> [-f|--force]    remove worktree (branch left intact)
  prune|p       [-y|--yes]             delete worktrees whose PR/MR is merged or closed
  rename|r      [name] <new> [-f|--folder]
                                       rename branch (optionally folder too)
  commit|cm     <name> [git-args]      commit -a in another worktree
  switch|s      <name|branch>          cd there (needs the shell wrapper)
  path          <name|branch>          print the path; for scripts
  current       [--json]               report the cwd's worktree + branch
  foreground|fg <name>                 pull another worktree's branch into cwd
  setup                                interactively configure options
  help|-h|--help                       show usage + resolved config
  __complete worktrees|branches        hidden: emit completion candidates

Worktrees are discovered from git's own registry (`git worktree list`), so any
worktree of the repo is recognized no matter where it sits. The layout —
auto-detected from the current repo — only decides where *new* ones are created:

  Bare layout: WT_ROOT contains a `.bare/` dir;
    new worktrees are direct children of WT_ROOT.

  Standard layout: WT_ROOT is the main worktree (git root); new worktrees go
    under WT_ROOT/.worktrees/<name>, which is kept self-ignored.

Settings live in git config under ryanmoelter-cli-tools.*, shared with stack, so each
one needs setting only once. A repo-local value overrides a global one, so
`--global` gives user-wide defaults:
  git config ryanmoelter-cli-tools.baseBranch staging   # default: origin/HEAD → "main"
  git config ryanmoelter-cli-tools.branchPrefix me/     # default: <local-part of user.email>/
  git config ryanmoelter-cli-tools.branchPrefix ""      # no prefix — branches use bare names
  git config ryanmoelter-cli-tools.forge gitlab         # github|gitlab|none; default: from origin URL
  git config ryanmoelter-cli-tools.nerdFont true        # Nerd Font glyphs; default: plain Unicode
  git config --add ryanmoelter-cli-tools.protectedWorktrees foo
                                              # repeatable; worktree folder names that
                                              # refuse delete/rename/foreground. .bare
                                              # and the main worktree are always protected
  git config --add ryanmoelter-cli-tools.ignoredPendingChecks "ci/slow job"
                                              # repeatable; CI checks whose perpetual
                                              # "pending" shouldn't mask the result

`wt setup` sets these interactively. Matching wt.* keys are still read and win
when set, for backwards compatibility only.
"""

